WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.360
"Shang-Chi and the Legend of the Ten Rings"&nbsp;
introduces a slew of new characters to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.960
Marvel Cinematic Universe. With all the new faces,&nbsp;
it's easy to miss the deeper significance of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.000
few of them. Here are all the characters from&nbsp;
"Shang-Chi" that are more than meets the eye.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.160
The Dweller-In-Darkness in "Shang-Chi"&nbsp;
is something of a counter dragon to The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:25.120
Great Protector. He has spent centuries&nbsp;
trying to break out of the dark gateway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.720
and finally managed to do so after tricking Wenwu&nbsp;
into breaking down the barrier with the Ten Rings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.760
He proceeds to suck up the souls of many&nbsp;
villagers and combatants before being defeated.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.880
The comic-book-version of the Dweller-In-Darkness&nbsp;
is a frequent villain of Doctor Strange as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:46.720
He's a Cthulhu-like demon with little physical&nbsp;
or biographical resemblance to what we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:51.760
in the "Shang-Chi" movie. In the film, he is&nbsp;
actually an alien who came to Earth long ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.280
bringing with him others of&nbsp;
his kind and the Ten Rings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.640
There's also a pretty decent chance that the&nbsp;
Dweller-in-Darkness is a reimagined Fin Fang Foom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.680
a diabolical dragon from the comics and a creature&nbsp;
that's popped up throughout the Marvel universe.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.440
Captain Marvel's appearance in the mid-credits&nbsp;
scene is, in many ways, traditional MCU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:17.440
worldbuilding. After spending almost the entire&nbsp;
movie doing their own thing, Shang-Chi and Katy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.560
finally meet up with Wong, Bruce Banner, and&nbsp;
Carol Danvers to discuss the Rings. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.840
obviously the opening salvo to future Shang-Chi&nbsp;
adventures and Avengers-style crossovers. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:33.200
it's not necessarily Carol who means more&nbsp;
than it appears — it's Brie Larson herself.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.680
Director Destin Daniel Cretton has four director&nbsp;
credits to his name prior to "Shang-Chi."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:43.840
Of those four, the last three, "Just Mercy,"&nbsp;
"The Glass Castle," and "Short Term 12" all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.400
feature Brie Larson. He's been there for many&nbsp;
phases of her career, with "Short Term 12"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:54.960
often listed as her first true dramatic lead, and&nbsp;
knew her well before she won an Oscar for "Room"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.200
or became Captain Marvel. That&nbsp;
makes "Shang-Chi" the fourth time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:02.800
they've worked together — even if this&nbsp;
particular appearance was a brief one.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:07.200
Villainous monster The Abomination&nbsp;
makes a brief appearance in "Shang-Chi,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.800
fighting Wong in Xialing's fighting ring.&nbsp;
This marks his first appearance in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.120
MCU since "The Incredible Hulk," and his&nbsp;
chummy relationship with Wong hints that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:19.680
this is just the start of Abomination's&nbsp;
new status in Marvel's shared universe.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:25.200
Even though he hasn't been seen on-screen in&nbsp;
more than a decade, Abomination has loomed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:30.640
the background for years. In the 2011 short "The&nbsp;
Consultant," Agent Phil Coulson said that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:36.640
powers that be wanted Abomination assigned to the&nbsp;
Avengers initiative. Coulson was against this and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:42.400
sent Tony Stark to tank negotiations. Abomination&nbsp;
was also name-dropped in an episode of "Agents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.120
of SHIELD," where the audience was informed&nbsp;
the one-time Hulk foe was still imprisoned.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.080
"I'll reassign you to Barrow, Alaska&nbsp;
and you'll spend the rest of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.800
years pulling the night shift&nbsp;
guarding Blonsky's cryo-cell."

00:02:53.520 --> 00:03:00.080
How and why Abomination ended up friendly with&nbsp;
Wong remains unknown. What is known is that Tim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:05.040
Roth is set to reprise the role of Abomination&nbsp;
in the upcoming "She-Hulk" Disney+ series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.400
As such, this part in "Shang-Chi" is more than&nbsp;
just a cameo — it's a sign of things to come.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:16.800
Though he's never named on-screen, and stays&nbsp;
silent through the picture's runtime, Death Dealer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:22.080
looms large over the proceedings of "Shang-Chi."&nbsp;
He's present for Shang-Chi's grueling training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:27.920
as a child, and acts as Wenwu's right hand man&nbsp;
later in life. He's rewarded for this loyalty by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:33.280
getting eaten up by a so-called "soulsucker,"&nbsp;
a victim of his boss's misguided quest.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:37.600
Though it's barely explored on screen,&nbsp;
Death Dealer does have a brief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.080
albeit notable comics history. Death&nbsp;
Dealer was a lieutenant of Fu Manchu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:46.800
Shang-Chi's father in the comics. He was&nbsp;
brutal, and willing to do anything to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.400
advance his position. Fu Manchu is the one who&nbsp;
provided Death Dealer with the mask and weapons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:56.880
Death Dealer also had an obsession with playing&nbsp;
cards, hence the name. He came to his end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.360
just a few issues after his introduction,&nbsp;
with Shang-Chi burning the villain alive.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:04.240
Though Death Dealer himself has&nbsp;
stayed dead all these years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:09.200
he did manage to leave something&nbsp;
behind: a son. In 2009, a "Shang Chi:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.560
Master of Kung Fu" one-shot comic had Shang-Chi&nbsp;
facing off against Huo Li, the Junior to Death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:20.320
Dealer's Frank Grimes. Might we see Huo Li in&nbsp;
further MCU adventures? Only time will tell.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:25.120
If you haven't read the comics or weren't&nbsp;
paying all that close attention in the theater,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.760
the name "Razor Fist" should make the&nbsp;
character in question easy to identify.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:35.520
A mercenary with a razor for a fist, he's hired&nbsp;
by Wenwu to track down Shang-Chi. The two have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.920
several altercations before ultimately&nbsp;
working together to stop the soulsuckers.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:43.760
There have been three Razor Fists&nbsp;
in the comics. The first appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:48.080
in a three-issue "Master of Kung&nbsp;
Fu" arc in 1975. Unlike the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:53.120
he had razors on each hand. He died after&nbsp;
his brief run, meaning that he arguably got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:57.440
more development over the course of a two&nbsp;
hour movie than he ever did in the comics.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:04:59.520
"I LOST MY HAND!"

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:05.520
The second and third Razor Fist debuted in&nbsp;
1981, in the form of brothers William and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.560
Douglas Scott. The two were in a car accident,&nbsp;
with each losing an arm. The villainous Carlton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:16.480
Velcro gave each of the Scott brothers a sword arm&nbsp;
and — not long after — shot and killed Douglas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:20.240
William has since bounced around&nbsp;
as a minor villain and henchman.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.800
It's not known which of the three possible&nbsp;
candidates is the Razor-Fist we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:29.520
in the "Shang-Chi and the Legend of the Ten&nbsp;
Rings' movie, but his survival means perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:34.240
we could learn his true identity and&nbsp;
backstory in some future MCU project.

